1.0  Introduction

1.1  General

Lords of  Irongate  is a  full  featured semi-random  adventure  game  with
hundreds of monsters, items, and  dungeon levels and has been  specifically
designed to be  run as  a BBS  door game.  The dungeon  itself is  randomly
designed and created  as the player  enters each new  level. As the  player
digs deeper  into the  depths  of the  dungeon  the treasures  become  more
plentiful and the monsters become more powerful. Despite its random  design
there is a fixed goal or objective to the game, that goal being to find and
retrieve the  magical orb  hidden deep  in  the depths  of the  dungeon  of
milina.

For anyone who's  ever played an  adventure game before  this game is  self
explanatory, for  those who  are new  to  adventure games  - The  Lords  of
Irongate contains a  friendly on-line user  interface. This user  interface
contains a reference list of available keypresses at  every prompt, and has
on-line command documentation as well as  hints with special tricks on  how
to complete the adventure.

This manual  assumes  you are  familiar  with  your Amiga  system  and  the
AmigaDOS operating system,  if not,  please refer to  your system  manuals.
Some keyboards have a 'RETURN' key in the place of a 'ENTER' key (they  are
identical) and in all cases this manual  will refer to this key as a  ENTER
key.


1.2  System Requirements

The Lords of  Irongate (Amiga  Version) will work  on any  Amiga 500,  600,
1000, 1200, 2000, 3000, 4000 system with a minimum of 2 megabytes of memory
running AmigaDOS v2.04  or higher  with at  least 1  megabyte of  available
space. Although, we recommend a minimum of 2 megs, it may run under smaller
memory configurations since the programs have been designed to utilize less
than 256k at any  one time in  order that it  may run while  a BBS is  also
loaded.

Lords of Irongate v2.xx contains some enhancements over the previous  v1.xx
versions but  still requires  a  BBS system  which  is capable  of  running
standard DOS type doors. A DOS  door, unlike a door specifically  developed
with hooks for a BBS, communicates  with the Standard Input Output  (stdio)
ports for all inputs and outputs and expects the BBS to pick-up and deliver
these messages  accordingly. This  allows  the DOOR  to    be  tested from  a
AmigaDOS console,  and it  allows it  to operate  on a  significanly  large
number of different types of BBS's.   Typical types of BBS which allow  for
DOS doors include DLG BBS, C-NET BBS and EXCELSIOR! BBS. There are numerous
others  -  please  check  your   BBS  documentation  prior  to   attempting
installation.


