3.3  Listing and Deleting Old Players

If you  have forgotten  the name  of  your player,  or you  want to  see  a
complete list of players in the players.dat  file, you can do so by  typing
the word  LIST instead  of the  players  name. During  the display  of  the
players you can kill any player simply by typing K for Kill (or delete) and
then entering the players correct name and password or the sysop  password.
Remember the sysop password can only be set from the command line  argument
SYSPW=xxx when you run the Irongate program.


3.4  User Commands Available During Gameplay

While the  game  is running  there  will be  a  general list  of  available
keypresses at the bottom  of the screen  or at the  current prompt. A  more
complete list and more detailed description is given when the user  presses
H for HELP  with commands.  The complete list  of commands  is as  follows:
(Remember to have NUM LOCK set for keypad movement!)
                                                          +-----+
[MOVE=KEYPAD] = Use the keypad keys to move as follows:   7 8 9
(+) Up a level  (pressing the '5' key will allow you       \|/ 
(-) Dn a level   to rest your character at your current   4- -6
                 location gaining back some health pts)    /|\ 
(O)pen/close  = Open or Close a door at direction         1 2 3
(A)ttack!     = Will attack a monster in the current area +-----+
(F)lee away!  = Allows you to run away from combat.



(X)Examine    = Will more closely examine the room contents and
                search the area for any hidden and/or secret rooms.
(I)nventory   = Will list a complete inventory of your holdings
(P)ick up item= Picks up an item in the current area
(D)rop item   = Drops an item you may be holding (or have
                readied)
(V)iew stats  = Displays a detailed view of your statistics.
($)ave player = Saves the current player holdings & stats to disk
(R)ead item   = To read scrolls, books, or activate magical
                effect of.
(U)se item    = Activate magical effect of rods, wands, and
                staffs.
(W)ear/wield  = Before your weapon or armor items are considered 
                'readied' they must be worn or wielded.
(S)peak with  = Allows you to talk to a wandering player or
                monster
(M)ix items   = Mix up two or more potions (can have random
                effects)
(T)ake off    = Allows you to take off (deselect) the currently
                worn or wielded item for another one
(C)ast spell  = If you have spell casting ability, use this
                command to activate your spell casting ability
(E)at/drink   = Wandering the dungeon will make you hungry, make
                sure to stop often and get something to eat/drink
(Z)Redraw!    = Will redraw screen (in case something got messed
                up
(Q)uit        = Quit this fabulous adventure & exit out...





4.0  Game Play Tips

There is a text file called  'LOIHINTS.TXT' (and an ANSI equivelant  called
'LOIHINTS.ANS) which contain additional tips on the game. This text file is
available for the players to read  during game play by simply entering  'H'
for help and then selecting the menu option for game tips.





5.0  Technical

5.1  File Description

The files which MUST be installed on your hard drive for game play (In  the
same directory!) include:

  IRONGATE           <- The main executable file for the game
  CHANGES.TXT        <- Irongate revision and release history info
  IRONGATE.DOC       <- This documentation file
  MONSTERS.DAT       <- Monsters data file
  ITEMS.DAT          <- Items data file

  LOICOVER.ANS       <- ANSI COLOR text Cover picture
  LOIHELP.ANS        <- ANSI COLOR text online Help document
  LOIHINTS.ANS       <- ANSI COLOR text online Hints document
  LOIABOUT.ANS       <- ANSI COLOR text online About document
  LOIHIST.ANS        <- ANSI COLOR text on the History of Irongate

  LOICOVER.TXT       <- TEXT (NO ANSI) Cover picture
  LOIHELP.TXT        <- TEXT (NO ANSI) online Help document
  LOIHINTS.TXT       <- TEXT (NO ANSI) online Hints document
  LOIABOUT.TXT       <- TEXT (NO ANSI) online About document
  LOIHIST.TXT        <- TEXT (NO ANSI) The History of Irongate

Additional files  which are  created as  needed by  The Lords  of  Irongate
include:

  PLAYERS.DAT        <- File containing the players information
  HISCORES.ANS       <- ANSI COLOR text with the top 10 scores
  HISCORES.TXT       <- TEXT (NO ANSI CODES) of the top 10 scores

Other files which may  be included in an  archive (DEMO) and/or  registered
disk copy but are not needed for the game to run:

  README.TXT         <- Last minute IMPORTANT! instructions
  FILE_ID.DIZ        <- Auto file identification file
  REGISTER.TXT       <- Registration form
  LOISTART           <- SAMPLE Startup Script file for some BBS's



5.2  The Data File Format

The Players file (PLAYERS.DAT) is a fixed field binary file containing  all
of the players statistics and items. It will expand each time a new  player
is created unless there are empty  (dead player) slots in which case  those
will be filled up first.

Though The Lords of  Irongate only uses about  4000 bytes for each  player,
and since  read/write  operations  are selective  to  the  specific  player
record, the size will have little  effect on game performance. However,  to
prevent the file from growing  too big with a lot  of players (ie.. if  you
had over 100 players then PLAYERS.DAT may get as big as 400k total) you may
want to  follow the  directions  in paragraph  3.3  to delete  old  players
[freeing up the slots].



Lastly, should a player have succeded in completed the quest - you may want
to level  the playing  field  and start  a whole  new  quest. This  can  be
accomplished by deleting  the PLAYERS.DAT and  HISCORES.* files. New  files
will be created as the opponents enter the dungeon.





6.0  Problems and Workarounds

6.1  Error handling

The Lords of Irongate  program contains a lot  of internal error  handling,
should an error occur  that was not properly  handled - please contact  the
technical support center (listed at the beginning of this document) with  a
problem report indicating the conditions, your version numbers, the  system
being used (both hardware, operating system and BBS) and any other  related
information needed to duplicate problem.


6.2  Incompatibilities

No incompatibilities have yet been identified.


6.3  Problems


6.3.1  Irongate can't find a file!

(A) All of the files listed in paragraph 5.1 must be located in the
    IRONGATE: directory for the game to properly run. Verify that
    all of the files are there.


6.3.2  Display is all garbled when Irongate starts

(A) Probable cause is the ANSI mode setting is either wrong, or the
    caller does not support both ANSI screen positioning and ANSI
    color codes. Try disabling ANSI COLORS with the ANSI=MONO
    argument. Remember the user MUST be able to use ANSI screen
    positioning codes else he won't be able to run the door.





7.0  Program Revision and Change History

The CHANGES.TXT contains the complete detailed revision and release history
of The Lords of Irongate.


7.1  Lords of Irongate Release 1.xx

Version 1.10+ of this adventure can be run from DOS (PC/MS-DOS or AmigaDOS)
for local use  AND since  it was specifically  developed to  use only  TEXT
characters (well  ANSI  characters)  with the  standard  input  and  output
devices (stdin,  stdout)  it  can also  be  run  on most  BBS  systems  for
competition amongst users.

Version 1.20+ Added VGA HI-RES GRAPHICS support for local use on PC
machines (Sorry, Amiga users). 


7.2  Lords of Irongate Release 2.xx

Version 2.00+ was developed reduce  the size (by over  50%) and to add  the
ability to be  run as  a BBS  door on the  IBM/PC Clone  type BBS  systems.
Compatibility was added for 7 of the most popular type of BBS DOOR designs,
see paragraph 1.2 for more info.  Version 2.01 fixed a bug introduced  into
v2.00.




